{% note
   text="Cron is a Linux job scheduler used to execute commands at specific times or intervals.\n\nBecause cron can execute commands automatically, attackers can abuse it to maintain recurring execution after gaining access to a system." /%}

### Cron Locations

{% twoColumn
   left="User crontabs are associated with individual accounts.\n\ncrontab -l\n\nDepending on the system, they are commonly stored under:\n\n/var/spool/cron/\n/var/spool/cron/crontabs/"
   right="System-wide cron jobs can be found in:\n\n/etc/crontab\n/etc/cron.d/\n/etc/cron.hourly/\n/etc/cron.daily/\n/etc/cron.weekly/\n/etc/cron.monthly/" /%}

{% terminal
   title="cron enumeration"
   commands=[{cmd: "crontab -l"}, {cmd: "sudo crontab -l"}, {cmd: "ls -la /etc/cron.d/"}, {cmd: "cat /etc/crontab"}] /%}

{% finding
   severity="informational"
   text="Suspicious Cron Jobs :\n\nLook for:\n• Jobs you don't recognize\n• Scripts stored in unusual locations\n• Commands running from /tmp or user-writable directories\n• Unexpected network commands\n• Jobs running with elevated privileges\n• Recently modified cron files\n" /%}

{% warning
   text="Don't Flag Cron Blindly\n\nCron is heavily used by legitimate system software.\nA scheduled job becomes interesting when its command, location, ownership, permissions, or execution context doesn't make sense for the system." /%}

{% tip
   text="Follow the Command\n\nFinding a suspicious cron entry is only the first step.\nFollow the command to the script or executable it launches, then inspect its ownership and permissions." /%}

{% callout
   text="PersistHunt\nCron is one of PersistHunt's persistence detection surfaces. The goal is to identify unusual scheduled execution and provide enough context for further investigation." /%}

### Example

{% terminal
   title="example cron entry"
   commands=[{cmd: "cat /etc/cron.d/example", output: "*/5 * * * * user /opt/scripts/backup.sh"}] /%}

This entry executes a script every five minutes. The schedule itself isn't malicious, so the next step is to inspect the referenced script, its ownership, permissions, and whether the job is expected.

### Takeaway

Cron persistence is simple but easy to overlook. During Linux enumeration, always check both user and system-wide scheduled jobs.
